.SH NAME
jotain \(em a custom GNU Emacs configuration built from scratch
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]just\f[R] \f[I]run\-built\f[R] | \f[I]run\-built\-debug\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]just\f[R] \f[I]build\f[R] · \f[B]just\f[R] \f[I]check\f[R] ·
\f[B]just\f[R] \f[I]fmt\f[R] · \f[B]just\f[R] \f[I]update\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[I]Jotain\f[R] is Finnish for \(lqsomething\(rq \(em a GNU Emacs 31
configuration (floor: Emacs 30.1) with no framework underneath.
Instead of layering on Doom or Spacemacs, it is built from scratch: Nix
builds the editor from source, plain Elisp configures it.
.PP
The repository ships both a modular Elisp configuration
(\f[I]early\-init.el\f[R], \f[I]init.el\f[R], \f[I]lisp/init\-*.el\f[R])
and the Nix expressions that build Emacs itself (\f[I]emacs.nix\f[R],
\f[I]overlay.nix\f[R], \f[I]default.nix\f[R], \f[I]flake.nix\f[R]).
The dev shell provides tooling only \(em Emacs is not in it; \f[B]just
run\-built\f[R] builds the editor via Nix and launches it with this
configuration.
